<languages />
<translate>
<!--T:1-->
Most people never need this page. [[<tvar name="1">Special:MyLanguage/Installation</tvar>|Installation]] offers a prebuilt binary and a prebuilt image, and either is a shorter road to a built site than this one. Build from source to run a change of your own, or a commit that no release carries yet.

<!--T:2-->
To run an unreleased commit without building anything, there are nightly builds: a dated <code>nightly-YYYY-MM-DD</code> image tag, and a nightly release of the [[<tvar name="1">Special:MyLanguage/Standalone binary</tvar>|binary]].

<!--T:3-->
Docker builds both products, the binary included -- the static PHP it embeds is compiled inside a container -- so the host needs only [<tvar name="1">https://git-scm.com/</tvar> git] and [<tvar name="2">https://www.docker.com/</tvar> Docker].

<!--T:4-->
<span id="the-image"></span>
== The image ==

<!--T:5-->
Clone the repository and build it:
</translate>

<syntaxhighlight lang="shell" copy>
git clone https://github.com/chaotic-ground/wikven.git
cd wikven
docker build -t wikven .
</syntaxhighlight>

<translate>
<!--T:6-->
That leaves a local <code>wikven</code> image, which you run exactly as [[<tvar name="1">Special:MyLanguage/Getting Started</tvar>|Getting Started]] runs the published one: only the name differs.

<!--T:7-->
<span id="the-binary"></span>
== The binary ==

<!--T:8-->
The [[<tvar name="1">Special:MyLanguage/Standalone binary</tvar>|standalone binary]] embeds the image's own tree, so build the image first, out of the same checkout. Then:
</translate>

<syntaxhighlight lang="shell" copy>
docker build -f binary.Dockerfile --target export -o type=local,dest=out .
</syntaxhighlight>

<translate>
<!--T:9-->
<code>out/wikven</code> is the executable. <code>--target export</code> stops at the last stage of <code>binary.Dockerfile</code>, which holds the compiled binary and nothing else, and <code>-o</code> writes that stage to a directory instead of loading it as an image.

<!--T:10-->
Expect it to take far longer than the image did: it compiles a static PHP from source and links FrankenPHP around the embedded tree. The workflow that builds the released binaries allows the step an hour.

<!--T:11-->
What comes out is built for the architecture you built it on, and runs from wherever you put it:
</translate>

<syntaxhighlight lang="shell" copy>
WIKVEN_WORKDIR=. ./out/wikven build
</syntaxhighlight>

<translate>
<!--T:12-->
[[<tvar name="1">Special:MyLanguage/Standalone binary</tvar>|Standalone binary]] covers installing it onto your <code>PATH</code>, and the host programs it uses when they are there.

<!--T:13-->
<span id="a-github-rate-limit"></span>
=== A GitHub rate limit ===

<!--T:14-->
The static PHP build resolves the libraries it compiles through <code>api.github.com</code>, which answers an address that sends no token 60 times an hour -- shared, on a company network, with everyone behind it. A build that stops there wants a token, which the [<tvar name="1">https://cli.github.com/</tvar> GitHub CLI] will print for an account you are already signed in to:
</translate>

<syntaxhighlight lang="shell" copy>
GITHUB_TOKEN=$(gh auth token) docker build -f binary.Dockerfile --target export \
  --secret id=github-token,env=GITHUB_TOKEN -o type=local,dest=out .
</syntaxhighlight>

<translate>
<!--T:15-->
It is passed as a build secret, so no layer of the image records it.
</translate>

